CORE
main.cpp
--cpp17
^\[main\.assertion\.1\] line \d+ global NSDMI applied: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.2\] line \d+ global NSDMI first member: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.3\] line \d+ global NSDMI second member: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.4\] line \d+ global NSDMI with a non-NSDMI member: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.5\] line \d+ non-NSDMI member zero-initialized: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.6\] line \d+ file-static NSDMI applied: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.7\] line \d+ WRONG: must fail: FAILURE$
^VERIFICATION FAILED$
^EXIT=10$
^SIGNAL=0$
--
^CONVERSION ERROR$
--
N5008 [class.default.ctor]/3 + [basic.start.static]: a default member
initializer (NSDMI) gives a class a non-trivial default constructor, so a
value-less namespace-scope / file-static object must run that constructor and
receive its declared member defaults.  CBMC previously treated such an object as
a plain POD in cpp_typecheckt::static_and_dynamic_initialization and merely
zero-initialized it (member left at 0 instead of its NSDMI value), because
cpp_is_pod ignores NSDMIs.  Fixed by routing a value-less definition whose class
has a direct NSDMI to the default-constructor path (which already applies NSDMIs,
as local objects show).  Members without an NSDMI keep their static
zero-initialization ([basic.start.static]/2), and objects with an explicit
initializer are unaffected (the initializer overrides the NSDMI).  assertion.7
provides non-vacuity.  Note: nested (member-subobject) NSDMIs and array-of-NSDMI
globals remain separate open gaps.
